{{also|Bein|be-in|bein'}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|been}}, {{l|en|bene}}
* {{l|en|bien}} {{qualifier|Scotland}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|enm|been}}, {{m|enm|beene}}, {{m|enm|bene||gracious, generous, pleasant}}, of unknown origin. Perhaps from {{der|en|non|beinn||straight, right, favourable, advantageous, convenient, friendly, fair, keen}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*bainaz||straight}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰeyh₂-||to hit, beat}}.

Cognate with {{cog|sco|bein}}, {{m|sco|bien||in good condition, pleasant, well-to-do, cosy, well-stocked, pleasant, keen}}, {{cog|is|beinn||straight, direct, hospitable}}, {{cog|no|bein||straight, direct, easy to deal with}}. See also {{l|en|bain}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/biːn/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-bein.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{hmp|en|bean|been}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|Now|_|chiefly|dialectal}} [[wealthy|Wealthy]]; [[well-to-do]].
#: ''a '''bein''' farmer''
# {{lb|en|Now|_|chiefly|dialectal}} Well provided; [[comfortable]]; [[cosy]].

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|beinly}}

